	"decision-auth-door": "yogurt takes the terminal's door and has no anonymous reader. The original required an invitation and a login before anything; the terminal publishes passkey and guest, and auth.required: true gates every screen. Rejected: a hand-written sign-in with an e-mail field (demanding what the runtime does not offer is a compile error), and a public browse mode (yogurt never had one). Accepted losses: a crawler sees the door; a guest identity lives in sessionStorage and a passkey is what brings a person back to their friends"
	"decision-communities-screen": "The directory is ten sections, the search is a row, the create form is on the page. A category is a message key on the row (decision 9), so browsing by category is ten public reads filtered on a word, in the order the 2004 directory listed them, and a category with no community draws nothing rather than an empty band. The search box is relational like everything else: one tab row per browser tab, upserted by the form, with the results region nested in it reading name=ilike.*{q}*, a snapshot read that re-runs when the row changes; the results panel is hidden by an attribute bound to q, so a blank search draws nothing. Creating a community is a form on the directory and not a page of its own, because the 2004 form was three fields and the new row's home is the section it lands in. Accepted: no paging per section (the demo's largest category holds three; a section past fifty earns a limit and a \"mais\"); search submits on a tap, not on each keystroke (a keystroke search is a machine and a round trip per letter for a list this size). Rejected: a route parameter for the search (a search is not an address anyone keeps); a category filter as a route (ten routes for one read each); a picture field on the create form (it belongs to the community's own page, where the owner edits it). Also decided: a second community with the same name is allowed, as the original allowed its thousand \"Eu odeio acordar cedo\"; the hits are capped at twenty with no \"mais\" until a search returns more than that in the demo, which none does; a search of spaces or of a wildcard (%, *) lists what it matches, on a public list, and is not worth a handler; this box is the directory's own filter and Buscar (a later turn) is the global search over people and communities; \"my communities\" stays on início, where it already is; every community name is a link to its page, and a category lists newest first, so the community a reader just made is the first row of its section, one tap from the page it made; a create form cannot navigate to a row whose id the client minted after the tap, which is why the creator is not carried there. The search folds case and not accents: \"cafe\" misses \"café\", as the original did; an unaccented search column is a generated column the brief does not ask for yet. Cost, as the architect seat simulated it: the ten section reads coalesce into one request and the member counts into another, but each row's picture read repeats its photo_url key and so does not coalesce, one request per community on every screen that draws faces; the blank search still reads its twenty hidden rows. Two seams for the platform's pending list: the coalescer could keep repeated non-equality keys, and the category word lives in the CEL, the form's options, ten filters and ten option values with only the CHECK enforcing it, which a lint over equality filters and option values would catch"
	"decision-community-page": "Membership is read through the me row; the forum's form is drawn for members and guarded for everyone. Membership is public, as the member lists were, so \"am I a member\" is the same detour the profile takes (decision 16): a probe nested in my Me row filtered on my id and the route's id, no embed, and the leave form row-scoped inside the probe's row. The new-topic form is drawn only when that probe has a row, which is a courtesy and not a rule: the rule is the 017 guard (decision 7), which refuses a non-member's insert with the code the form already paints as a refusal, so a hand-made request meets the same wall as a hidden form. Leaving was two taps until turn 11 and is one, since rejoining is free (decision 23). The owner may leave; the community stays, owned, with no members, as the original allowed, and nothing here deletes a community, and 011 grants no delete arm on it, since a delete would cascade through every member's topics and posts: an ownerless-of-members community is accepted until a moderation turn, and the demo holds none. Editing a community — its description, its picture — is the owner's and arrives with the editing turn beside editar perfil; no topic is deleted or moderated yet. Members are capped at twelve with the count in the band and a members page since turn 11 (decision 23); topics are newest first with no paging, the demo's largest forum holding two; a topic's page, where replies live, is topic, and every title links there. Rejected: a derived \"member\" column on the community row (a pipeline round trip for a probe the row already answers); hiding the forum from non-members (the forum was public reading). Two things the record states rather than fixes: the armed leave is ephemeral, a details the next paint closes, so a reload lands on \"sair da comunidade…\" with nothing deleted; and the members and the count are server-durable, so another reader's join reaches an open page on its next read, not live, which is the trade the profile's counts make too"
	"decision-debts": "The debts: paid in the mechanisms that owed them. A community's creator is its first member because the create path's trigger (022) mints the membership, not because a screen remembers to. The opener deletes a topic through a DELETE arm behind a guard that refuses while a reply exists, the opener's own included, so the control the reply count hides is never the rule, and across readers that count is as fresh as the page's last read, since post is a server table; the guard is a trigger, not a policy clause, because it runs after the row is locked and so sees a reply that committed first, and it lets a cascade through (one trigger level deeper) so 005's meaning for a leaving person or community is kept. Its refusal sentence is generic, since the control is hidden while replies exist and a failed delete is then more likely the network's. A face box binds its own row's picture, the embed carrying photo_url, instead of a nested read per face: thirty-three regions gone and the reads they cost with them (decision 14 holds the mechanism); 011 is kept byte for byte although its comment points forward to 022, because the edge's ledger refuses a shipped script that changed; a null link binds as the binder's transparent pixel, a data: src that fetches nothing, and the letter disc keys on it. The nested one-row reads of a person that stay are each a row the screen does not hold: início's own card and the asker in its requests; the writer in the profile's testimonial teaser and in the testimonials page's waiting and approved lists; the community page's owner; the edit form's own row. Each is a live list that carries no embed, or the screen's own subject, and the face binds from that row once it is read; the person probes beside them are probes, not reads of a face. A count is one ICU message whose plural the interpreter picks, so \"1 amigo\" needs no handler. \"salvo\" is the screen's own success stamp"
	"decision-demo-network": "A fresh yogurt comes populated. 020 inserts fifteen fictional people with friendships, scraps, approved testimonials, fans, ratings, twelve communities with members, and fifteen forum threads with replies, as a raw migration with fixed uuids and ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING. A social network with nobody in it cannot be used, let alone judged; a migration rather than seed: because the rows are user-generated content a database holds, and fixed ids because tests and fixtures name them. Every person, place and sentence is invented"
	"decision-design-identity": "The identity is Scrapbook (DESIGN.md): the pale-blue page, white panels with light-blue title bands, blue links, and yogurt's pink spent on commitment alone. Chosen over the 2009 redesign and over a feed-era card stream, because the brief names the artifact and a reader who was there must recognize it in the first second. Both appearances carry the same token names; the dark twin lowers value and keeps hue"
	"decision-door-split": "The door is the terminal's gate markup dressed as a split page, after roda. The terminal renders the gate — intro, name, hint, error, passkey, guest, providers — and ships no style; the app's shared stylesheet is on that page too, so it lays the intro and the form out as a header-blue panel beside the form on the pale ground, with the panel's question and its line as the page's own words per language, since no catalogue reaches the gate. The alternative, a login screen of the app's own, is refused by the prelude (the terminal owns sign-in); the default gate, unstyled, is a door that does not say what is behind it"
	"decision-driver": "The ledger's unreachable clauses are decided by a driver at every integrate, by screen tests over posed rows, and by the release's browser journeys; the karma levels are tap-to-set rows. The sprint 4 walk passed thirteen ids short of a clause no walk reaches: a forged write only a crafted request makes, a race only two sessions run, a cap the fifteen demo people cannot fill, a mean that needs three raters. Three instruments, each at the cheapest tier that can decide its clause. tests/ledger.ts is a build check, so it runs in the integrate closure against the runner's own stack with five guest sessions over /crud: a stranger's delete and a non-owner's update come back as nothing touched (the policy filters, the request succeeds), an outsider's reply, a blank body, a rating by a non-friend or of oneself, a second testimonial and a delete of a replied topic come back refused (a trigger or a CHECK raises), and the delete racing the reply is fired twenty times in both orders and never takes a reply with it. Those rounds are a smoke, not the proof: HTTP cannot pin an interleaving. The proof is at the SQL tier, in the lock order the turn's architect forced with two sessions and pg_sleep on a throwaway database: the row is locked before a BEFORE ROW trigger fires, the reply's foreign key holds the topic's key until the reply commits, and the volatile guard snapshots after the lock, so either the reply fails on a topic gone (017's refusal, or the key's) or the delete fails on a reply landed; the same guard written as a DELETE policy reads before the lock and loses, which is why 022 is a trigger (its comment pins VOLATILE). Each clause is a test step named by its acceptance id, so a failure reports per clause and the later ones still run. tests/screens.test.ts mounts the emitted screens on posed rows for what a photograph cannot judge and the demo cannot reach: the plural counts, the search cap, the saved stamp, and, off the shells, every capped region's limit and the karma rows' shape; the tap itself is a release journey, two guests made friends and a rating given through the chips. The guest strip at phone width, the saved word and the chips are judged by tests/verify.ts, the browser journeys every release is verified with, because the build check's runner image has no browser (recorded for the platform). The karma form draws each smiley as a row of four radio chips; the update form has no button, so a tap on a level is the save, the terminal submitting a buttonless form on change; the first rating keeps \"avaliar\" since it takes all three. The control vocabulary has no radio, so the program says select, its nearest word. Rejected: the three browser lines as a build check (no browser in the runner); the caps as seeded demo rows (a hundred-person network for a twelve-tile cap); mounting início and the profile whole for the caps and the rows, which the harness's select grammar refuses for an embed hinted by its foreign key column, as the feed's actor and the fan probe's idol are (recorded for the platform)"
	"decision-edits-and-members": "Editing is a form over the row that is already mine; leaving a community is one tap; members get a page, not a longer panel. The profile's edit form is the ownership primitive once more: my app_user row read through my me row, every control bound to its column with data-value, one row-scoped update, and 011's column grant the only rule, so the page needs no own-page probe and nobody edits anyone else's. The handle is not edited: it is the address of every page about me and the name every row that points at me carries, and a rename is a moderation turn's question. The same primitive edits a community: a Me probe on owner_id draws the form in the owner's page and the owner's policy admits the write, description and photo link alone, since the name is the community's address in the directory and the category decides its section. Leaving a community is one tap, by DESIGN.md's rule: two taps where getting back needs someone else's yes (unfriend) or where writing is destroyed (apagar), one where the reader can redo it alone, and rejoining is free; turn 8 made it two and recorded that this turn would make it one. A members page behind the count, not a longer panel, because the panel's twelve are a tease and a list of hundreds is a page of its own, the shape amigos already has; it reads Membership with an embed, safe there since it is server-durable and form-free. The strip already names the reader and leads to their page (decision 13), so the brief's \"own-name link\" was there since turn 1. A control the reader empties submits the empty string, which an optional column must not hold and the https rule refuses, so every optional control names the editing-text adapter, golaberto's, whose parse is trim-or-null: a cleared field is an unset column, on the date too, whose client normaliser refuses \"\". A seam the walk met: inside an item, a route link with no params and no other bound attribute is skipped before the pass that addresses links, since only elements with a bound attribute are visited, and the static pass never sees a template's contents; the two \"editar perfil →\" links bind their title so the pass reaches them. Recorded for the harvest. Accepted: no picture upload, pictures stay links (decision 9); no \"saved\" sentence, the row re-reading with my words being the confirmation. Rejected: editing inline on the profile (one page, two modes, every rule doubled); a rename of the handle; an owner deleting a community (decision 20 stands)"
	"decision-escape-hatches": "Escape hatches: none. Argued, not assumed. Everything here is schema, policy, trigger or screen markup: nineteen entities, twenty raw migrations (a declared seam, checked-in SQL a reviewer reads), fourteen screens and zero handlers. The emitted bayt.cue redeclares the cluster unchanged, which is what \"none\" has to mean to be checkable"
	"decision-faces-shipped": "The demo network's pictures are the app's own files, content-addressed, served as statics on every target. Decision 14 took pictures by link and named the risk: a host down or blocked draws a broken image, and the demo faces sat on two hosts the site did not own; the sprint 4 walk saw a letter where a picture should be. They are twenty-five files under pictures/ now, named by their sha256, three hundred and eighty kilobytes in the tree, declared as cluster statics the way doom declares its engine, so caddy serves /pictures/<file> on the local stack and the docker release and the edge bundle writes the same files into its assets: one meaning on three targets, offline, cached by content. The picture rule admits that exact shape beside https, a 64-hex name with its extension, so a reader cannot point a face at any other path; their own picture is still a link they paste (decision 14 stands for them). assets/manifest.json records each file's source, author and license, which the credits screen draws, since ten of them are Wikimedia Commons files under CC BY and BY-SA terms. A fifteenth person joins the network with her own scraps, a topic and friends, so the rail's cap and the newest-member states have a row beyond the fourteen, and the older threads gain a quiet topic, old scraps and a stranger's reply. Rejected, after the turn's architect: a blob seed from a public bucket (apexrun's shape), which turns on an open blob plane nobody writes, fetches at every launch and integrate, needs a bucket grant, and serves nothing on the docker target; data URIs in the seed (a migration the edge's ledger would carry forever); leaving the links"
	"decision-fans": "Fãs: the toggle on the profile, a stateless \"ser fã\" under every tile, the list behind the count. A fan row is public and its state for the reader is a probe on two keys, the person's and the reader's, and a binding resolves against its enclosing row alone: inside a tile the reader's id is a row away, so \"am I a fan\" cannot be asked per tile without a read per tile, the cost turn 13 just removed. So the tile carries the create and no state: one tap makes the reader a fan, \"você é fã\", a link to the profile where the one-tap undo lives, shows under the tile for a breath by the saved line's mechanism (the screen's success stamp, held by a delayed transition in the form that keeps focus, which a browser that does not focus a tapped button never grants: the one known hole, the terminal's to close), and the count moves where one is shown; a second tap is refused by the unique pair and the line in the tile says so while the screen holds the refusal, clearing with it, since nothing in the tile types; the reader's own tile hides it through the me probe, which is local. The tile is where a person \"appears\": people to meet, search, a friends list, a fan list, a community's members and the members page. Not the two panels of the reader's own circle on início (their friends, their fans), which are theirs to visit rather than to court, and not rows: a scrap, a testimonial or a reply carries its writer as a link and its own gestures, and a courting button there would compete with them. A fan of mine on my fans page still gets the button, since being a fan of a fan is the point. Undo is the profile's one tap. The toggle with its state stays on the profile, where the route's handle reaches the probe. The list is the friends page over fan rows, newest first, the fan as an embed on fan_id; início shows the reader's own fans the way it shows their friends. Rejected: a probe per tile through an inner embed on me (no key joins me to fan); fan as a live table so the probe is local (still two keys from one row); a handler (none, by decision 10). Decision 28 later proved a reader-relative policy as a fourth shape; it does not serve here, since the fans page needs everyone's rows and a second table of \"mine\" is the read per tile this decision declined"
	"decision-forum-membership": "A forum is its members'. A topic or a reply from outside the community is refused by a BEFORE INSERT trigger (017) with the code the screen already reads as a refusal, because RLS cannot express \"a row of another table says yes\" without a helper, and a policy that reads membership under the writer's own RLS would read the public table anyway. The screen will hide the form behind a membership probe; the trigger is the backstop"
	"decision-friendship-mirror": "A friendship is two rows, minted by a trigger. One row per direction makes a friends list one filter on user_id with the friend as an embed, and a friends count a projection over that same read; a single undirected row would need an OR over two columns and a template that cannot say which embed is \"the other person\". 015 mints both edges when the addressee sets accepted and removes the request; deleting either edge removes its mirror. No browser INSERT arm exists on friendship, so the trigger is the only writer. Rejected: a materialized view (not a table, so it would not sync) and a pipeline (seconds of delay for a tap the person is watching)"
	"decision-inert-first-turn": "The first turn is knowingly inert. A fresh guest lands on a card with three zeros, a rail of people, and profiles that offer nothing to do; their own profile is blank with no road to editing yet. That is the plan, not an oversight: the controls arrive on the screens that own them — befriend and fan on perfil (turn 3), scraps (turn 4), requests end to end (turn 5), testimonials (turn 6), communities (turns 7–9), the reader's own form (turn 10) — each as a form over an entity declared here, and the own-profile frame that names what is missing and where to fix it is drawn with the form it points at. Until then the demo network is what sells the next click"
	"decision-karma": "Karma: the rating is the rater's private row, the smileys are the cluster's derived row. A rating is three levels, trustworthy, cool and sexy, zero to three, one row per rater per friend, private to the rater (decision 2): on a friend's profile the reader reads their own row for that person on the friendship row's key, the update form lives in that row, and the create form beside it is drawn only once the region has stamped that there is none. The levels cross a value adapter as numbers. The smileys everyone sees are the rounded mean of every rater's row and how many rated, which no reader's own rows can supply, so the cluster keeps them: one KarmaSmiley row per rated person, public and live, written by a definer trigger on karma and by nothing a browser holds (023 takes back the standing grants); live because trigger-born rows reach a server region only on its own client's next write (decision 4), and a rating must move the smileys at once. The level a screen draws is a message key the row carries, the {msg[col]} idiom, so a glyph is the catalogue's. The row exists from three raters up, by the refresh itself and not by a screen's filter: with one, the public row would be that one friend's exact vote; with two, a rater who knows their own levels could solve for the other; from three the row is a mean, and what remains (a live change the rated person can date, the count moving by one) is accepted and named here, as orkut's was. Only a friend rates, by a guard on the row, a rating leaves with the friendship, and a rating is undone in one tap, by DESIGN's redo clause: the three levels are the reader's to enter again. The refresh is callable by the trigger alone: PostgREST publishes every function at /rpc with EXECUTE to PUBLIC by default, and 023 takes that back for this one and, by the global default (a per-schema default undoes only a per-schema grant), for every function after it. The residual the sprint's architect named is larger than three raters hides: a rater who steps their own level through 0 to 3 and watches the public mean flip learns the other raters' sum, and an unfriend's drop in the count dates the leaving friend's vote. That is accepted as the price of a live mean, named here and left: the sprint 5 architect measured that a refresh on the ticker would not close the stepping channel at any cadence nor the count's drop, and a floor of five with hysteresis would only blur them; the residual stands as the design's. Rejected: a view over karma (the program declares tables, and a view would be a second grammar for one read); the mean computed by the client (it cannot see the rows); a pipeline box for one aggregate a trigger keeps exactly"
	"decision-live-relations": "The three relation tables are live, and a relation is read as my own rows, each probing the page's person. A server region re-reads after this client's own write to its entity and never otherwise, so the friendship rows the 015 trigger mints on an accept were invisible to the accepter until a reload, and the asker's \"pedido enviado\" outlived a decline. Friendship, FriendRequestSent and FriendRequest now ride the sync shape: the public one reaches everybody (its list was public already), the private halves reach their one party, as Me does. The probes also change shape: a read whose filter or select goes through an embed (friend.handle=eq., a !fk hint) is routed to PostgREST; on a live table it does update, by re-reading on every change to the table anywhere in the network, and its rows never enter the local collection, so a row-scoped write on one of them finds nothing to write (início's \"recusar\" said so in the playtest). So each relation is a list of my own rows with no embed — my friendships, my sent requests, my inbox — served from the collection, and every row nests a one-row probe on AppUser filtered on that row's other party and the route's handle; :has() over the probe picks the row that is this page's, and that row carries its own undo, row-scoped. Cost: the friendship collection is lazy, so a client loads the rows its views ask for, but every client receives every friendship change in the network from the moment a page opens, so the term that grows is the network's write rate times open tabs, not the table; and each probe is one PostgREST read of a server row at mount, so a reader with N friends spends N reads on a profile and the arms hold until the last lands. The same rule reaches forward: the three testimonial tables and Fan are server today, and an approval or a fan written on one row and mirrored or counted on another will show its mirror on the next read only, so the turns that build depoimentos and fãs make those tables live and read their probes as my rows with a person probe, in the same edit as the screen. The tile lists that keep an embed (friends, the profile's amigos, início's friends) are read-only and re-read on any profile edit in the network; the price of a name beside a face until the select parser takes a hint. The pair unique is ordered, so a request to a friend or across one already waiting the other way is refused by a 015 guard, and an accept clears the reverse pair too. Início's pending panel is the same inbox list without the probe, and it carries no embed: a region read with an embed comes through PostgREST, and a live table's row that arrived that way is not in the collection a row-scoped write resolves against, so its \"recusar\" found no row to delete (seen in the playtest, fixed by reading the asker through a nested region on their own key); it sits under the reader's card because an unanswered request is the one thing on início that asks something of the reader, and it shows its sentence when empty so the place is learned before it is needed. There is no \"sent requests\" panel: what I asked for is read on each person's page, as it was. A withdrawn request leaves the panel under the reader's finger, because the half is live and there is nothing left to answer. The profile shows twelve friends and início nine: one row of tiles more where the page is the person's. Friends are newest first on both, with no sort to choose; the original had none either. The strip carries no pending count: notifications are outside the brief. Rejected: polling a server region (a clock for a fact the shape already carries); keeping the embed filter and refreshing on a timer"
	"decision-me-row": "Me: a live, private row per person, provisioned synchronously. The binding grammar has no session placeholder, so the only way a screen can ask \"is this mine\" or \"which row is mine\" is a table RLS has already narrowed to one row. A trigger inside the auth service's insert (013) rather than a pipeline, because a guest must not wait seconds for their own card to exist; read-only to browsers (012) so nobody deletes themselves out of their own page"
	"decision-message-keys-as-values": "Relationship and category are stored as message keys. {msg[relationship]} renders the row's own value through the reader's catalogue, so \"namorando\" and \"dating\" are one stored word, the CEL enum closes the set, and a select control's options are the same keys. Rejected: storing the Portuguese word (untranslatable) and an integer code (a second mapping the screen would have to carry)"
	"decision-news": "Novidades: a feed table the cluster writes, read under a reader-relative policy. The brief decided a view unioning three public tables; the program declares tables and has no view grammar, and turn 15 proved the shape that does the same work: a Feed row per public doing, written by definer triggers on scrap, testimonial and membership as each happens and removed with its source, backfilled once. What makes it the reader's is the policy 024 restates on the table: a row is visible when its actor is the reader's friend, so one server region on início with no filter but a cap reads exactly their friends' twenty newest doings. Server, not live: a reader-relative policy is no shape to sync, and the brief wants the panel fresh when início opens, not pushed. The sentence per kind is a message the row names by key, and the kind as a data attribute picks which link follows it, so three kinds are one row template. Nothing private enters: no draft, no request, no rating, and a testimonial is dated by the day it was written, since its approval lives only in the subject's private row; the cost is that one approved late sits under newer doings and, past twenty, out of reach, which \"ver mais\" in sprint 5 answers. The policy reads O(feed) for a reader whose friends did little; a fan-out inbox or an index by actor and time is the shape to revisit before the table nears a hundred thousand rows. Rejected: the view (no grammar; a second mechanism beside 27's); three regions, one per table, merged by the stylesheet (no order across them); a live table (the policy is per reader). Measured on the edge at the sprint 5 wrap: novidades is empty there for every reader. The edge serves a GET on a public-scope table as a shared read with no subject (isPublicRead, scopes public: alone), so auth_uid() is null inside the reader-relative policy and nothing passes it; the local stack's PostgREST always carries the subject, which is why the ledger driver and the walk passed. On the edge, visibility is a scope, not a policy: a row a reader may see must carry that reader's scope. The feed is the inbox now, shipped at the wrap: FeedItem, one row per reader and doing under user:<reader>, fanned out by 025 from the log for each friend of the actor as a doing lands, and for a new friendship the friend's hundred newest, removed with the doing and with the friendship; the log itself is the cluster's alone, and the screens read the inbox newest first. It answers the scale question too: a sparse reader's read is bounded by their own rows. The release's verify runs a feed journey first, a friend's scrap read on início and on the page, so the edge cannot go green again with novidades empty"
	"decision-pending-private": "Pending state is two private rows, one per party, kept equal by triggers — never a public row narrowed in SQL. This turn's first draft declared requests and unapproved testimonials public and replaced their SELECT arm with the two parties' own; the architect seat showed that PostgREST honours that and Electric does not: the sync path reads the WAL, applies no policy, and the auth service mints a shape token for any scoped table, so a third guest could stream every pending row. A row only two people may see must therefore be a row only their scopes carry: the asker's FriendRequestSent and the addressee's FriendRequest, the writer's TestimonialDraft and the subject's TestimonialPending, each private on its party, the second half written only by the 015 mirror (011 revokes its browser INSERT), and the one word each addressee may write — accepted, approved — held by a column grant. Accepting mints the friendship edges; approving inserts the public Testimonial; deleting either half removes the other. Rejected: private with a shared grant table (a third table per flow for the same two scopes), and a pipeline (seconds of delay for a tap the person is watching)"
	"decision-pictures-by-link": "Pictures are links, and a face and a letter are the two states of one box. The cluster carries no blob plane; a person's picture is a URL they supply (photo_url), as a community's is, and capabilities.resources admits image requests to any https host so a reader may paste any link. The demo network's faces are stock portraits by link, its communities' pictures seeded photos by link: a social network with no faces is dead, and faces that only arrive with uploads would have arrived in turn 15; since turn 18 the demo network's own pictures are the app's static files (decision 30), and this decision stands for the picture a reader pastes. Each picture is the row's own photo_url bound into the face's img (the embed carries the column where the row is a join), and a null link binds as the binder's transparent pixel, a data: src that fetches nothing; the letter disc shows exactly when the src is that pixel, so the choice is made by the bound value and never by an image's load: a missing link never draws a broken glyph. In a posed frame the fixture adapter blanks every src to the same pixel, so the storybook shows discs where the live page shows faces. A link whose host is down or blocked still draws a broken image, an accepted risk of pictures by link, named here, and the reason same-origin copies of the demo faces were on the plan and are decision 30. Until turn 13 each picture was a nested one-row region filtered on the link, the disc its :empty, which cost a read per face (thirty-four on a profile) and was replaced once the pixel was known (decision 25). Rejected: AI-generated portraits (a second generator to pin for the faces); same-origin files (deferred: they would make the demo offline-safe, and belong with the richer fake data of turn 18). The demo rows are edited in place rather than by an upgrade migration: 020 is applied by initdb on a fresh volume alone, and no database of this app exists outside a fresh volume yet; the day one does, demo content changes become upgrade migrations like any other (docs/schema-change-admission.md)"
	"decision-polish": "Polish is paid in the grammar the app already has. \"Ver mais\" past the twenty novidades is a page, news, the panel's row over the same policy with a cap of a hundred, because a cap that grows needs arithmetic no attribute has and a second route needs nothing. \"Para você\" is a me probe on the doing's subject, local and free, and a rule that swaps the name for the word. The arms under a row of tiles sit level because a name is always two lines tall, and each is the control height. The interests panel of a person who filled nothing is gone by a rule on its own empty cells; \"responder\" on one's own scrap by the writer's probe the row already carried. What polish did not get: a \"saved\" word that does not depend on the tapped button keeping focus, which is the terminal's stamp to give (on the pending list), and tap-to-set karma rows, which a radio group through a value adapter has not been proven to carry and stays with sprint 5's driver turn. What still depends on the tapped button keeping focus, after this turn: the \"ser fã\" arm under a tile and the \"salvo\" of the two edit forms; the rating's form confirms by showing the reader's own levels, focus or not. The arm's height is the design's control height, 36 px, under the 44 px floor some guides name and above the 24 px one the visual gate holds. The scrapbook frames that drew relative times now draw the datetime format, and every declared state has its frame, which lint holds"
	"decision-probe-empty": "Ownership and state are empty-or-not probes, and every control is a sibling of its probe, never its child. A region nested inside a probe's item template binds against the probe's row; a sibling keeps the enclosing row and is chosen by a :empty sibling combinator. A probe carries no whitespace inside it, since the sweep removes stale elements only and a text node would keep it non-empty forever"
	"decision-public-read-owner-writes": "Public content with owner writes is public plus additive raw policies, never a declared private widened in SQL. #Access has no \"everyone reads, the owner writes\" mode, and the browser mirror believes the declared mode; widening in SQL would make one table mean \"mine\" in the tab and \"everyone's\" on the server. 011 adds the arms — asker/addressee on FriendRequest, author/owner on Scrap, author/subject on Testimonial, fan, owner, member, author — and two column grants narrow what an UPDATE may touch (handle stays out of reach; a request's and a testimonial's only writable column is its acceptance). Narrowing is always safe under the mirror rule"
	"decision-relation-through-me": "A reader's relation to a person is read through the reader's own row. Friendship and Fan are public, as their lists were, so a probe filtered on the person's id answers \"does anyone\" rather than \"do I\", and the binding grammar carries no session placeholder. The one row that is mine alone is Me: the person row nests a one-row me region, and inside it the friendship and fan probes filter on {id} (mine) and {param.handle} (theirs, through an inner embed, so the read is server-computed). The forms that start a relation need the person's id, so they stay siblings in the person row, and :has() on the arms box joins what the probes know to which form is drawn. The forms that undo one live inside the probe row they undo: a filtered delete resolves its rows against the public table before the policy narrows it, so idol_id=eq.{id} on a person with other fans lands on another fan's row and deletes nothing (found in the turn-4 playtest). The two request halves are private to one party each and need no detour. Rejected: a per-reader derived table (a pipeline round trip for a tap the person is watching) and making friendship private (its list is public on every profile). Accepted and recorded: a decline is silent and the asker may ask again without limit, as the original allowed; a cooldown is a row nobody asked for yet"
	"decision-scrapbook": "The scrapbook is its own page; the profile teases it. The box to leave a scrap lives on scrapbook, above the list, and the profile's scraps panel is the newest three with the count and two links — the band's to read on, the foot's to the box — as the 2004 tab was, and because a profile with a textarea in it is a form, not a page. Newest first, because a scrapbook is read from the top for what is new and the old is scrolled to, not for. Who may delete is the 011 policy's OR — the writer, or the owner of the page — and the control is drawn for them alone by two one-row probes on the reader's Me row in each scrap's row, one on author_id and one on owner_id: the ownership primitive (decision 4) applied twice, no session placeholder, no derived column. Deleting takes two taps, the same arm-and-commit as unfriend, because a scrap is as gone as a friendship. A person may scrap their own page, as they could then; the demo network happens to hold none. The reply road is \"responder\" under every scrap, to the writer's own scrapbook, which is where the conversation lived. A scrap is never edited; it was not then, and an edit would need an updated_at the brief does not ask for. Accepted: no paging; the page is one list however long, and the demo's longest scrapbook is five; the first scrapbook past fifty earns a \"mais\" that this list's limit already knows how to grow. Rejected: a can_delete computed on the server (a round trip per row for a glance the client already has the rows for); the box on the profile (every profile visit would mount a form); hiding \"apagar…\" until hover (a phone has no hover). Scrap stays server, with the cost the sprint wrap named: a scrap reaches another reader's open scrapbook or início on their next read, and a writer whose scrap the owner swept meanwhile gets the refusal line on \"apagar\"; it has the network's highest write rate, which is what a live shape would carry to every tab"
	"decision-search": "One box in the strip, two lists under it, the directory's idiom. The 2004 top bar had one search that found people and communities; so does the strip here, on its own page, since the strip's own markup is the terminal's and holds no form. The page is the directory's search again: a tab row the form upserts, its q read by nested lists, the attribute bound to it hiding the panels while blank. People match on search_text, a generated column that is the display name and the username in one, because a person without a name is their username everywhere here and a second list would show the same person twice, and because an or filter is PostgREST's alone: the local matcher that serves a posed fixture has no or, while one ilike on one column it serves on both sides; a comma or a parenthesis typed into an or group would also have split it. The column is not named search_name, which the local matcher would fold for accents while the server's ilike does not, so a fixture would pass a query the live app fails; case folds on both sides, accents on neither, until the server carries a collation that folds them, which the edge's PGlite cannot yet; communities match on the name alone, since the description is prose. Both are a case-folded contains, ASCII case, as the directory's is; neither ranks. Accepted: no paging, one cap of twenty-four on each list, four rows of six tiles and as many rows, with the hint saying so and asking for a longer word when someone is missing, since by name the same early names always win a short query; the demo holds fifteen people and twelve communities, under the cap; no search of topics or scraps, which a search of posts would need a text index for. Rejected: a search box inside the strip (not the app's markup); separate people and community pages (one gesture, two answers); ranking (nothing here is ranked)"
	"decision-terminal-chrome": "The strip, the back stack, sign-out, the screen states, row motion, the unconfirmed-write badge and the storybook are the terminal's. No screen redraws them; the app dresses the strip in header blue through shell/shared/chrome.css and nothing else, and keeps the guest's strip by offering a passkey (auth.promote): the terminal draws no strip for a guest it offers no way in to, and the brief's name-beside-sign-out needs one"
	"decision-testimonials": "A testimonial is a friend's word, one per person, approved before it is seen; the three tables go live. The 2004 product had it so: only a friend could write one, you approved it before it appeared, and one per friend was the norm. The 021 guard holds the friend rule and the no-second-after-approval half where a hand-made request meets them, with the same SQLSTATE as the row CHECK; a unique pair on the draft and on the public table holds one-per-person under concurrency, where a guard's read cannot; the invariant from turn 1 refuses oneself; and an unfriend takes the unapproved ones with it in 015, since the rule is a friend's word. The guard runs as the caller: its reads are public tables and the caller's own drafts, and a definer would answer a stranger's hand-made insert naming another writer with a sentence that reveals a private draft, so 011 also stops granting author_id and from_id, which default to the token and which no form sends. The pending state stays two private rows kept equal by 015 (decision 6), and the three tables become live for the same reason the request halves did in turn 6: the subject's copy and the public row are a trigger's rows, and a server-durable region re-reads only after this client's own write, so without live the subject would reload to see a draft arrive and the writer to see the approval. Because they are live, no list here carries an embed: a row that arrives through an embed-path read is not in the collection a row-scoped write resolves against (decision 16), and every row on this page carries a form. The writer is therefore a nested region on their own key, the início pedidos idiom. The profile's teaser reads the same way. The communities panel sits under the portrait, where the original kept it, and reads Membership with an embed, which is safe there: server-durable and form-free. Início gains one line, not a panel: an approval is a decision about a sentence and belongs on the page that shows the sentence, so the line counts and links. Accepted: no paging; the writer may not edit a draft, since 011 revokes UPDATE so the subject never approves text the writer no longer shows; a withdrawn or discarded one is gone, not kept. Rejected: approving from início (two places to do one thing); a testimonial without friendship (the original's rule, and the guard is four lines); an embed on a live list (the turn-6 lesson)"
	"decision-topic-page": "The route carries the community so the probe can; replies are the writer's to delete, and nobody's to edit. A membership probe nested in the reader's Me row can filter only on its own columns and the route's params, so the thread's address is /comunidade/:community/topico/:id: the community's id rides in the route, the probe reads {param.community}, and a title's link carries both ids. Only the first segment translates, through the community's own slug, as the address reads as a path under the community. Replies are oldest first, because a thread is read top-down and the box sits at its foot, where the next reply goes. A reply is the writer's to delete in two taps, as a scrap is, and nobody's to edit; the topic itself has no delete arm in 011 since the sprint 3 wrap, as the community has none: a delete cascades through every member's replies, which are theirs; the opener deletes a topic with no replies through 022's guard (decision 25), and a moderation turn decides the rest. The topic's community is read through an embed in the select, which routes the one-row read through PostgREST; it is a name beside a title and never written to, so the cost is a read on every change to topics, which is what the forum's own list already pays. Accepted: no paging of replies, the demo's longest thread holding four; Post stays server-durable, so another reader's reply reaches an open thread on its next read, as the forum's list does, the live tables being the three relation tables alone. Rejected: a membership column on the topic row (a derived fact for a probe the me row answers); a flat route /topico/:id with the community read from the row (the probe cannot read a sibling region's column)"
}

